; ======================================================================
;  mailbox.pi4 - the VideoCore mailbox, property channel, on a Pi 4
; ======================================================================
;
;  THE FIRST LIBRARY FOR THIS TARGET, and it is the first for a reason:
;  on a Raspberry Pi the ARM does not own the machine. The VideoCore
;  firmware owns the clock tree, the power domains, the framebuffer, the
;  thermal sensor and the board's identity, and the ARM asks for every
;  one of them down this one wire. Anything later that needs a clock
;  rate, a power rail or a display buffer comes back here.
;
;  ---- A QUICK EXAMPLE ---------------------------------------------
;
;    XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"
;
;    Procedure Main()
;      Define hz.i
;      If MailboxInit() = 0
;        ; the buffer is outside the window the VideoCore can reach -
;        ; see THE 1 GiB DMA WINDOW below
;        ProcedureReturn
;      EndIf
;      hz = MailboxArmClock()          ; what the CPU is running at NOW
;      hz = MailboxArmClockMax()       ; the ceiling the firmware allows
;      Repeat
;      ForEver
;    EndProcedure
;
;  ---- HOUSE RULE 10: THIS LIBRARY INCLUDES NOTHING ------------------
;  There is no XIncludeFile line below. A library never includes another
;  library; the MAIN file lists what it needs, one line each, in order.
;  This file happens to need nothing at all - no clock setup, no UART,
;  no timer. It reads CNTPCT_EL0/CNTFRQ_EL0 directly for its timeouts
;  precisely so that it has no dependency to declare and can be the
;  first thing a program calls.
;
;  That is also why its counter helpers are called MbxTicks() and
;  MbxCounterHz() and not Ticks() and TickHz(). RaspberryPi4/Lib/
;  timer.pi4 owns those two names and reads the same two registers. A
;  main file that includes both must not find the name defined twice,
;  and this file must not be the one that quietly requires the other -
;  so it keeps its own two-instruction copies under a private prefix.
;  Every internal in this file is Mbx*; everything public is Mailbox*.
;
;  Include paths are ROOT-RELATIVE ("RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"),
;  never "../..".
;
; ======================================================================
;  WHERE EVERY NUMBER IN THIS FILE COMES FROM
; ======================================================================
;
;  THE VC MAILBOX IS NOT IN THE BCM2711 PERIPHERALS MANUAL. Chapter 13
;  of that manual, "ARM Mailboxes", is a DIFFERENT device: the sixteen
;  core-to-core mailboxes in the ARM_LOCAL block. Grepping the manual
;  for "b880" returns nothing. So none of the transport numbers below
;  can be cited to it, and pretending otherwise would be the exact
;  failure this project keeps paying for. They are cited to the two
;  sources that DO describe this device and that are on this machine:
;
;    Datasheets/pi4/bcm283x.dtsi          the device-tree node
;    Datasheets/pi4/bcm2711.dtsi          the soc ranges / dma-ranges
;    Datasheets/pi4/bcm2835-mailbox.c     the mailbox driver
;    Datasheets/pi4/raspberrypi-firmware.c the property-channel driver
;    Datasheets/pi4/armstub8.S            CNTFRQ_EL0 on this part
;
;  ---- THE ADDRESS -------------------------------------------------
;  bcm283x.dtsi:100    mailbox: mailbox@7e00b880 { reg = <0x7e00b880 0x40>; }
;  bcm2711.dtsi:41     ranges = <0x7e000000 0x0 0xfe000000 0x01800000>
;
;  Bus $7E000000 maps to ARM $FE000000, so the node's $7E00B880 is ARM
;  $FE00B880. The node is $40 bytes long, which is worth noticing: it
;  covers $FE00B880..$FE00B8BF, i.e. BOTH mailboxes, which is what makes
;  the mailbox-1 offsets below part of the same documented block.
;
;  ---- THE REGISTERS -----------------------------------------------
;  bcm2835-mailbox.c defines them as offsets from that base:
;
;    ARM_0_MAIL0 0x00   ARM_0_MAIL1 0x20
;    MAIL0_RD  = MAIL0 + 0x00     $FE00B880   ARM reads replies here
;    MAIL0_STA = MAIL0 + 0x18     $FE00B898   status of the READ side
;    MAIL1_WRT = MAIL1 + 0x00     $FE00B8A0   ARM writes requests here
;    MAIL1_STA = MAIL1 + 0x18     $FE00B8B8   status of the WRITE side
;
;    ARM_MS_FULL  = BIT(31)       $80000000
;    ARM_MS_EMPTY = BIT(30)       $40000000
;
;  and the comment above them states the direction plainly: "We deliver
;  to the VC in mailbox 1, it delivers to us in mailbox 0."
;
;  >>> THE ONE PLACE THIS FILE DISAGREES WITH THE COMMON TUTORIAL CODE.
;  Nearly every bare-metal mailbox example on the internet polls FULL on
;  $FE00B898 - mailbox ZERO's status - before writing to mailbox one.
;  The driver does not: bcm2835_last_tx_done() reads MAIL1_STA ($38) for
;  FULL and only the receive path reads MAIL0_STA ($18) for EMPTY. A
;  full mailbox 0 means "there are eight replies the ARM has not read",
;  which has nothing to do with whether there is room to SEND. The
;  tutorial version works in practice because that condition is almost
;  never true, not because it is the right question. This file asks the
;  right one, and if MAIL1_STA turns out to read back wrong on silicon
;  the symptom is a bounded #MBX_ERR_TXTIMEOUT and not a hang - which is
;  the whole reason every wait here has a deadline. FIRST THING TO CHECK
;  ON THE BENCH if MailboxSend() times out with nothing else wrong.
;
;  ---- THE CHANNEL AND THE 16-BYTE ALIGNMENT ------------------------
;  raspberrypi-firmware.c:21-24
;
;    #define MBOX_MSG(chan, data28)  (((data28) & ~0xf) | ((chan) & 0xf))
;    #define MBOX_CHAN_PROPERTY      8
;
;  One 32-bit word carries both: the low four bits are the channel and
;  the upper 28 are the address. That is WHY the buffer must be 16-byte
;  aligned - there are no low four bits left to put an address in. The
;  firmware wiki says the same thing in one sentence: "The buffer itself
;  is 16-byte aligned as only the upper 28 bits of the address can be
;  passed via the mailbox."
;
;  Alignment is computed AT RUN TIME here, not assumed. The emitted .bss
;  aligns __bss_start__ to 16 but packs the globals after it eight bytes
;  at a time, so a global array's own base is 8-aligned at best and can
;  be worse if some earlier file declared an odd-sized byte array. The
;  buffer below is over-allocated and MailboxInit() rounds an aligned
;  window out of the middle of it.
;
;  THE WINDOW IS ALIGNED TO 64, NOT 16, AND THAT IS A CACHE DECISION
;  RATHER THAN A MAILBOX ONE. Sixteen is all the mailbox word needs. But
;  the buffer is cleaned and invalidated by CACHE LINE, a line on a
;  Cortex-A72 is 64 bytes, and cache maintenance cannot be aimed at half
;  of one. A 16-aligned buffer shares its first and last lines with
;  whatever globals the compiler happened to place either side of it -
;  including this file's own procedure locals, which are statically
;  allocated in the same .bss. That sharing is not untidy, it is a
;  CORRECTNESS BUG waiting to happen: touching a neighbour between the
;  send and the reply makes the shared line dirty with a stale copy of
;  the buffer, and the clean that follows the reply would write that
;  stale copy back OVER the firmware's answer. Aligning the window to 64
;  and sizing it to exactly one line means the line belongs to the
;  buffer alone and nothing else can dirty it. The array is 128 bytes so
;  that a 64-byte line always fits inside it whatever the base turns out
;  to be.
;
;  ---- THE BUS ADDRESS, AND WHY THE CONVERSION EXISTS ---------------
;  bcm2711.dtsi:45     dma-ranges = <0xc0000000 0x0 0x00000000 0x40000000>
;
;  The address handed to the mailbox is NOT an ARM address. The ARM and
;  the VideoCore see the same DRAM through different windows: what the
;  ARM calls $00000000 the VideoCore calls $C0000000. The mailbox word
;  is consumed by the VideoCore, so it must carry the VideoCore's name
;  for the buffer - ARM address + $C0000000. Hand it an ARM address and
;  the firmware reads some other megabyte entirely and replies with
;  nonsense or not at all; nothing faults, because $3E000000 is real
;  memory from the VideoCore's point of view too.
;
;  ---- THE 1 GiB DMA WINDOW -----------------------------------------
;  That dma-ranges entry is 0x40000000 LONG. The alias covers exactly
;  the low gigabyte: ARM $00000000..$3FFFFFFF. There is no bus alias in
;  this window for anything above 1 GiB, so a mailbox buffer placed
;  there CANNOT BE NAMED to the VideoCore at all - "+ $C0000000" would
;  wrap past 32 bits and the truncated word would point somewhere else.
;
;  This is a live hazard on this target, not a theoretical one: the
;  Monitor's high payload window starts at $40000000, and an image built
;  with --load-addr 0x40000000 puts its .bss (load base + 512 KiB) and
;  therefore this buffer straight into the unreachable region.
;  MailboxInit() CHECKS FOR THIS AND REFUSES, with #MBX_ERR_ADDRESS,
;  rather than sending an address that means something else. Build the
;  program that talks to the firmware for the low window.
;
;  ---- THE PROPERTY BUFFER ------------------------------------------
;  raspberrypi-firmware.c:92-116 builds it, and the firmware wiki
;  documents the same layout:
;
;    +0   u32  total size of the buffer in bytes, padding included
;    +4   u32  request/response code
;    +8   u32  tag id
;    +12  u32  value buffer size in bytes
;    +16  u32  request: 0        response: b31 set, b30-b0 = length
;    +20  ...  the value words
;    ...  u32  end tag, 0
;
;    RPI_FIRMWARE_STATUS_REQUEST = 0
;    RPI_FIRMWARE_STATUS_SUCCESS = 0x80000000
;    RPI_FIRMWARE_STATUS_ERROR   = 0x80000001
;    RPI_FIRMWARE_PROPERTY_END   = 0
;
;  The three status values and the tag numbers further down are NOT in
;  any file on this machine - raspberrypi-firmware.c uses the symbols
;  and the header that defines them, include/soc/bcm2835/raspberrypi-
;  firmware.h, is not here. They were read out of that header in the
;  kernel tree and CROSS-CHECKED, value by value, against the firmware
;  project's own "Mailbox property interface" documentation, which is
;  the upstream both of them describe. Two independent sources agreeing
;  on every number is the bar this file was held to; see THE TAGS below
;  for what was deliberately left out for failing it.
;
; ======================================================================
;  THE TAGS THIS FILE IMPLEMENTS, AND THE ONES IT DOES NOT
; ======================================================================
;
;  IMPLEMENTED - each of these appears with the same hex value in BOTH
;  the kernel header's enum rpi_firmware_property_tag AND the firmware
;  project's property-interface documentation:
;
;    $00010002  get board revision       req 0   resp 4
;    $00030002  get clock rate           req 4   resp 8
;    $00038002  set clock rate           req 12  resp 8
;    $00030004  get max clock rate       req 4   resp 8
;    $00030007  get min clock rate       req 4   resp 8
;    $00030047  get clock rate, MEASURED req 4   resp 8
;    $00030006  get temperature          req 4   resp 8
;    $0003000A  get max temperature      req 4   resp 8
;
;    clock ids: EMMC 1, UART 2, ARM 3, CORE 4, V3D 5, H264 6, ISP 7,
;               SDRAM 8, PIXEL 9, PWM 10, HEVC 11, EMMC2 12
;    Only the four this file names as constants were needed; the rest
;    are listed here so the numbers are written down once and can be
;    passed to MailboxClockRate() directly.
;
;  NO LONGER LEFT OUT - THE FRAMEBUFFER FAMILY. This entry used to read
;  "deliberately left out, for want of a second source", and it said of
;  "$00040001 and its neighbours" that the family "deserves its own
;  library, written against a display on the bench, not a guess appended
;  to this one". Both halves have since been overtaken by events and the
;  text is corrected rather than deleted, because the next reader who
;  greps this header for a framebuffer tag needs to know it is here and
;  not go looking for the library that was promised:
;
;    the nine framebuffer configuration tags are carried below, under
;      THE FRAMEBUFFER TAGS, each cited to two on-disk headers. The
;      "whole protocol" worry was right - see SEVERAL TAGS IN ONE
;      MESSAGE for what it cost to learn - but the answer was a
;      multi-tag transport here plus RaspberryPi4/Lib/display.pi4 on
;      top of it, not a second mailbox.
;    the three DISPLAY SELECTION tags ($00040013 / $00048013 /
;      $00040016) are carried below as well, added 2026-08-26, and they
;      are the reason this section needed rewriting at all: they are
;      literally "neighbours of $00040001" and a reader who trusted the
;      old sentence would have concluded this file had decided against
;      them. It had not. Nobody knew they existed, because neither of
;      the two headers cited for everything else contains them. See
;      THE DISPLAY SELECTION TAGS below for the third source and why it
;      took a third one.
;
;  DELIBERATELY LEFT OUT, for want of a second source or of a use:
;
;    the POWER DOMAIN tags ($00020001 / $00028001). Cited fine, but
;      turning rails off with no consumer of them is a way to make a
;      board stop working, not a feature.
;    get board serial / model / MAC ($00010004 / $00010001 / $00010003),
;      get voltage ($00030003), get throttled ($00030046), turbo
;      ($00030009 / $00038009), ARM/VC memory split ($00010005 /
;      $00010006). All are cited by the same two sources, and any of
;      them can be issued TODAY through MailboxBegin/MailboxSend
;      without editing this file - see THE ESCAPE HATCH. They are not
;      wrapped because nothing has asked for them yet.
;    GET_DISPLAY_SETTINGS ($00040014). Its NUMBER is as well cited as
;      the three display tags next to it - the same enum, the same
;      line block - but a tag number with no known payload is not a
;      usable tag, and its payload is the one thing no source on this
;      disk describes. The downstream header carries a bare
;      "#define GET_DISPLAY_SETTINGS_PAYLOAD_SIZE 64"
;      (raspberrypi-firmware-downstream.h:185) and says nothing about
;      what those sixty-four bytes contain; the property-interface
;      documentation does not mention the tag at all; and
;      vc4_firmware_kms.c, which is what taught us the other three,
;      never sends it. So the number is written down below as a
;      comment, where it is findable, and no constant is declared for
;      it - because declaring one would invite somebody to send it and
;      read the reply as though the layout were known.
;    anything whose number could only be recalled rather than read.
;      There is none of that in this file. If a tag is not above, it is
;      not because it was forgotten.
;
;  ---- THE ESCAPE HATCH ---------------------------------------------
;  MailboxBegin / MailboxSetWord / MailboxSend / MailboxWord are public
;  on purpose. The transport is the hard, verifiable part and the tag
;  number is one word the caller can supply. That is what keeps an
;  un-wrapped tag from being a reason to guess at a wrapper:
;
;      If MailboxBegin($00010004, 0, 8) <> 0 And MailboxSend() <> 0
;        lo = MailboxWord(0) : hi = MailboxWord(1)   ; board serial
;      EndIf
;
; ======================================================================
;  SEVERAL TAGS IN ONE MESSAGE - ADDED 2026-08-26, AND WHY IT HAD TO BE
; ======================================================================
;  Until today this file could only build a message carrying ONE tag,
;  and the single-tag call above was described as the whole story. It is
;  not, and the gap cost a working display.
;
;  WHAT HAPPENED. RaspberryPi4/Lib/display.pi4 asked the firmware for a
;  framebuffer with seven separate single-tag messages - set physical
;  size, set virtual size, set depth, set offset, allocate, get pitch.
;  Booted through U-Boot it worked, every time. Booted STRAIGHT FROM THE
;  FIRMWARE, with nothing having configured video first, DisplayInit()
;  returned success and produced a 2 x 2 framebuffer with a pitch of 64,
;  while the panel on the bench is a perfectly good 1920 x 1080 and a
;  GET of the physical size said so.
;
;  It was chased as a timing problem first - the mode "had not settled
;  yet" - and that theory is dead: ten attempts spread over 1.8 seconds
;  returned 2 x 2 every single time. Waiting longer for a request that
;  is shaped wrong is superstition.
;
;  THE CAUSE. The firmware accumulates the framebuffer's requested state
;  WITHIN ONE PROPERTY TRANSACTION and allocates against what that one
;  transaction has accumulated. Separate messages do not build on one
;  another for this purpose, so the allocate ran against a default and
;  handed back a placeholder surface. Under U-Boot the symptom was
;  invisible because U-Boot had already done the combined call and the
;  firmware had a real mode configured before our seven messages
;  started.
;
;  THE AUTHORITY, and it is on this disk:
;    Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_msg.c:35-47   struct msg_setup - ONE
;      buffer, NINE tags, then the end tag.
;    Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_msg.c:159-204 bcm2835_set_video_params()
;      fills all nine and makes ONE bcm2835_mbox_call_prop() call, then
;      reads the physical size, the pitch, the framebuffer address and
;      the framebuffer size back out of THAT SAME response.
;    Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_mbox.h:31-36 says it plainly in prose:
;      "With some limitations, an arbitrary set of tags may be combined
;      together into a single message buffer."
;
;  SO THIS FILE NOW COMPOSES. MailboxListBegin / MailboxListAdd /
;  MailboxListSetWord / MailboxListSend / MailboxListWord are the
;  multi-tag half of the transport. The single-tag four are UNCHANGED -
;  a great deal calls them and none of it was touched - and the two
;  halves share one buffer, one alignment argument, one set of cache
;  operations and one bounded wait, because duplicating the transport
;  would have been two places to get the mailbox wrong.
;
;      If MailboxListBegin() <> 0
;        t0 = MailboxListAdd($00048003, 8, 8)    ; set physical w/h
;        t1 = MailboxListAdd($00040008, 0, 4)    ; get pitch
;        MailboxListSetWord(t0, 0, 1920)
;        MailboxListSetWord(t0, 1, 1080)
;        If MailboxListSend() <> 0
;          w = MailboxListWord(t0, 0)
;          p = MailboxListWord(t1, 0)
;        EndIf
;      EndIf
;
;  MailboxListSend() checks the response code AND every tag's own
;  response bit, not just the first, and MailboxListFailedTag() names
;  the index of the first one the firmware left unanswered. A list where
;  eight tags were understood and the ninth was not is a FAILURE here,
;  reported as #MBX_ERR_TAG, because the eight that worked configured a
;  display that the ninth was supposed to allocate.
;
;  ONE DELIBERATE DIFFERENCE FROM THE SINGLE-TAG PATH, stated because it
;  looks like an inconsistency and is not. In a request, each tag's
;  third header word is the "value length". The kernel writes ZERO there
;  for every tag (raspberrypi-firmware.c:170, req_resp_size = 0) and the
;  single-tag path below matches the kernel. U-Boot writes the size of
;  the REQUEST half instead (v2025.01_mbox.h:94-98,
;  BCM2835_MBOX_INIT_TAG sets val_len = sizeof(body.req), and :100-104
;  BCM2835_MBOX_INIT_TAG_NO_REQ sets it to 0 for a pure get). Both work
;  on real firmware - each of the two drivers is in daily use - and the
;  list path follows U-BOOT, because U-Boot is the code whose combined
;  framebuffer message is known to work on this board and reproducing it
;  byte for byte removes one variable from the next thing that goes
;  wrong. The single-tag path is not changed to match: it is proven as
;  it stands and nothing here is worth risking for symmetry.
;
; ======================================================================
;  CACHES, AND WHY THIS FILE CLEANS THE BUFFER EVEN THOUGH IT MIGHT NOT
;  HAVE TO
; ======================================================================
;  The VideoCore reads the buffer out of DRAM. It is not a coherency
;  master with the ARM's D-cache. So if the D-cache is on, the words
;  this file writes can still be sitting in it when the firmware looks,
;  and the reply can still be in DRAM when this file reads the cache.
;
;  Whether the cache IS on depends entirely on who started the program:
;
;    the firmware, as kernel8.img  -> MMU and caches OFF (which is what
;                                     the emitted _start assumes)
;    a resident loader, via "go"   -> whatever the loader left enabled,
;                                     and U-Boot on AArch64 runs with
;                                     MMU and caches ON. "go" is not
;                                     "bootm"; it does not tear them
;                                     down for you.
;
;  A library cannot know which. "dc civac" on a line that is not cached
;  costs a few cycles and does nothing, so this file cleans and
;  invalidates the buffer unconditionally, before the send and after the
;  reply, and puts a dsb around the mailbox accesses. Correct in both
;  worlds beats correct in the one that happened to be tested.
;
; ======================================================================
;  64-BIT LANGUAGE MODEL - THE TRAPS THIS FILE IS WRITTEN AROUND
; ======================================================================
;  * .i AND POINTERS ARE EIGHT BYTES on this target. A bare Peek/Poke is
;    therefore a 64-BIT access. Every mailbox register and every word of
;    the property buffer is THIRTY-TWO bits, so every access in this
;    file is spelled PeekL/PokeL. A bare one would be a 64-bit access to
;    a 32-bit register and it would not announce itself.
;  * A 32-BIT READ DOES NOT AGREE WITH ITSELF ABOUT SIGN. Verified in
;    the emitted assembly of this compiler: PeekL lowers to a bare
;    "ldr w" (zero-extending), while reading a .l ARRAY ELEMENT lowers
;    to "ldr w" followed by "sxtw" (sign-extending). Two spellings of
;    the same width with opposite answers for bit 31. This file
;    therefore MASKS with & $FFFFFFFF at every point where bit 31 can be
;    set and the value is then compared or returned - the response code
;    $80000000, the echoed mailbox word, and any clock rate at or above
;    2.147 GHz. Masking is right under either lowering, so nothing here
;    depends on which one you get.
;  * & | ! BIND TIGHTER THAN << >>. Every shift in this file is
;    parenthesised.
;  * >> IS ARITHMETIC on a signed .i. The only right shift here is on a
;    physical address, which is positive.
;  * PROCEDURE LOCALS LIVE IN THE INVOCATION'S FRAME. This line used to
;    say they were statically allocated; that stopped being true on
;    2026-09-11 and the correction matters here more than most places,
;    because it is the half of the reentrancy story this file owns that
;    CHANGED. The other half did not: there is still ONE shared property
;    buffer (mbxBuf) and one shared tag list, so this library is still not
;    reentrant, and the rule is unchanged - do not call into it from an
;    interrupt that can land on top of a MailboxSend(). What changed is
;    only that the locals are no longer part of the problem.
;    A consequence worth stating: mbxBuf is file-scope and always was,
;    which is why the VideoCore may hold its address across a call. A
;    property buffer built in a local would now be a frame the firmware
;    reads after the call that made it has returned.
;
; ======================================================================
;  WHAT HAS BEEN VERIFIED - 2026-08-24
; ======================================================================
;  Compiles clean, with no warnings, under BOTH compilers present in the
;  tree: pmfc_64.exe, which implements the 64-bit model this file is
;  written for, and the shipped pmfc.exe, which at the time of writing is
;  still the older 32-bit build. Gate:
;
;    pmfc scratchpad/mbxprobe/mbxtest.pi4 -t pi4 --load-addr 0x200000
;         -o scratchpad/mbxprobe/mbxtest.img -s
;
;  The emitted assembly was READ, and confirms:
;    $FE00B880 / $FE00B898 / $FE00B8A0 / $FE00B8B8 all materialised as
;      written, each reached with a 32-bit "ldr w" or "str w",
;    the +$C0000000 bus conversion emitted as an add of $C0000000
;      immediately before the or-with-8 and the "str w" to the write
;      register,
;    the buffer window rounded up with "+63 & $FFFFFFFFFFFFFFC0" - a
;      full 64-bit mask, not a truncated one - and the array emitted as
;      ".space 128",
;    the 1 GiB window check comparing bufAddr+64 against $40000000,
;    the response code compared against $80000000 after an explicit
;      32-bit mask, and the tag's response bit tested separately,
;    "dc civac" and the dsb/isb barrier assembled.
;
;  ---- 2026-08-26, THE MULTI-TAG HALF -------------------------------
;  MailboxListBegin / Add / SetWord / Send / Word were added and are
;  exercised end to end by tools/a64/a64_display_check.py: the real
;  pmfc.exe builds
;  RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4DisplayProbe.pi4, the image runs
;  on the A64 oracle, and a modelled VideoCore PARSES THE WHOLE TAG LIST
;  out of the buffer rather than assuming one tag. That model checks the
;  structure and stops the run rather than answering politely if the
;  size word, a tag's value-buffer length, the end tag or the request
;  code is wrong - so the composed buffer's layout is verified by
;  something that did not compose it.
;
;  The nine-tag framebuffer message goes out as one transaction, all
;  nine response bits come back set, and every value is read out of that
;  one reply. The gate's --mutate rebuilds display.pi4 seven ways -
;  including the exact seven-message shape that produced the 2 x 2
;  screen - and every one of them goes red.
;
;  THE SINGLE-TAG PATH WAS RE-RUN, not assumed unaffected: the clock,
;  SDIO/CYW43 and HCI gates all still pass, and each of them drives
;  MailboxBegin/MailboxSend through this file.
;
;  ---- VERIFIED ON SILICON. The list below used to be the "NOT
;       VERIFIED, AND ONLY SILICON CAN" list, and silicon did. --------
;
;  THE FIRMWARE ANSWERS. Proven 2026-08-25 and in continuous use since;
;  RaspberryPi4/Monitor/anvil.pi4 includes this file and calls it at
;  every boot, so a broken mailbox is a board that does not come up.
;
;    * MAIL1_STA IS THE RIGHT REGISTER TO POLL FOR FULL. The transaction
;      completes; it would hang or drop if it were not.
;    * REPLY LATENCY sits comfortably inside the one-second deadline -
;      no mailbox timeout has ever been reported at the bench, across
;      the clock, framebuffer, EMMC-clock, MAC-address and SDIO paths.
;    * WHAT THE FIRMWARE DOES WITH A SET ON THE ARM CLOCK, which was the
;      one flagged "above all". IT CLAMPS AND REPORTS SUCCESS. Asking
;      for more than the part allows returns response code SUCCESS with
;      the tag marked answered and the rate silently limited, which is
;      why every setter in RaspberryPi4/Lib/clocks.pi4 returns THE RATE
;      THE FIRMWARE APPLIED rather than the rate that was requested. On
;      this board ClockInit() takes the ARM from 600000000 to a measured
;      1500345728.
;    * THE FULL CLOCK TABLE, read back off the board 2026-08-25, Pi 4
;      Model B rev 1.2, 4 GB:
;
;          clock  configured      min          max        measured
;          ARM     600000000   600000000   1500000000    600169920
;          CORE    200000000   200000000    500000000    200008304
;          EMMC    250000000   250000000    250000000    250000496
;          UART     48000000           0   1000000000     48001464
;
;      The UART row is corroboration rather than a reading: it matches
;      config.txt's init_uart_clock=48000000 AND a console visibly
;      working at 115200, so the mailbox and the serial port confirm
;      each other.
;    * THE NINE-TAG FRAMEBUFFER TRANSACTION reaches a real firmware and
;      brings up a 1920x1080 surface on the direct-boot path - the path
;      that had never been tested, because under U-Boot the surface
;      already existed and the old seven-message version could not fail
;      visibly.
;
;  ---- STILL NOT VERIFIED --------------------------------------------
;
;    * WHETHER THE D-CACHE IS ON UNDER THE LOADER IN USE. Still open,
;      and now sharper rather than vaguer: the MMU has been enabled on
;      this board (2026-08-26) with DRAM mapped write-back cacheable,
;      and every run so far turns translation off again before
;      returning. A resident program that leaves caches on has not used
;      this file.
;    * EVERY TAG'S BEHAVIOUR. The tags this tree actually sends are
;      exercised; the rest of the tag space is transcription with a
;      citation attached.
;
;  ---- SUPERSEDED -----------------------------------------------------
;
;      ~~Nothing in this file has run on a Pi 4.~~
;
;  This was the first library written for this target and one of the
;  first proven, and the sentence outlived its truth by two days while
;  every clock number quoted anywhere in this tree came through here.
; ======================================================================

EnableExplicit

; ---- the device -------------------------------------------------------
; bcm283x.dtsi mailbox@7e00b880, translated by bcm2711.dtsi's soc ranges.
#MBX_READ    = $FE00B880       ; MAIL0_RD   - replies arrive here
#MBX_STATUS0 = $FE00B898       ; MAIL0_STA  - the READ side's status
#MBX_WRITE   = $FE00B8A0       ; MAIL1_WRT  - requests go here
#MBX_STATUS1 = $FE00B8B8       ; MAIL1_STA  - the WRITE side's status

#MBX_FULL    = $80000000       ; ARM_MS_FULL  = BIT(31)
#MBX_EMPTY   = $40000000       ; ARM_MS_EMPTY = BIT(30)

#MBX_CHANNEL = 8               ; MBOX_CHAN_PROPERTY
#MBX_CHANMASK = $F             ; MBOX_MSG puts the channel in the low nibble

; ARM address -> VideoCore bus address. See THE BUS ADDRESS above.
#MBX_BUS_OFFSET = $C0000000
; ... and the end of the window that offset is valid in.
#MBX_DMA_LIMIT  = $40000000

; ---- the property protocol -------------------------------------------
#MBX_REQUEST    = 0            ; RPI_FIRMWARE_STATUS_REQUEST
#MBX_SUCCESS    = $80000000    ; RPI_FIRMWARE_STATUS_SUCCESS
#MBX_END_TAG    = 0            ; RPI_FIRMWARE_PROPERTY_END
#MBX_RESP_BIT   = $80000000    ; set by the firmware in the tag's +16 word
#MBX_LEN_MASK   = $7FFFFFFF    ; and the length is what is left of it

; Byte offsets inside the buffer.
#MBX_OFF_SIZE   = 0
#MBX_OFF_CODE   = 4
#MBX_OFF_TAG    = 8
#MBX_OFF_VALLEN = 12
#MBX_OFF_TAGCODE = 16
#MBX_OFF_VALUE  = 20

; The same layout said the other way round, for the multi-tag path,
; where a tag can start anywhere. The buffer header is two words and
; each tag header is three; the six offsets above are these two plus
; these four, and they are written out separately rather than derived
; from each other so that a reader of either path sees the whole shape
; without arithmetic.
;   v2025.01_mbox.h:69-72  struct bcm2835_mbox_hdr  { buf_size; code; }
;   v2025.01_mbox.h:88-92  struct bcm2835_mbox_tag_hdr
;                          { tag; val_buf_size; val_len; }
#MBX_HDR_BYTES    = 8
#MBX_TAGHDR_BYTES = 12
#MBX_TOFF_ID      = 0          ; the tag number
#MBX_TOFF_VALLEN  = 4          ; the value buffer's size in bytes
#MBX_TOFF_CODE    = 8          ; request: length in; response: b31|length
#MBX_TOFF_VALUE   = 12         ; the value words

; ---- the tags (see THE TAGS above for the two sources) ---------------
#MBX_TAG_BOARD_REVISION = $00010002
#MBX_TAG_GET_CLOCK      = $00030002
#MBX_TAG_SET_CLOCK      = $00038002
#MBX_TAG_MAX_CLOCK      = $00030004
#MBX_TAG_MIN_CLOCK      = $00030007
#MBX_TAG_CLOCK_MEASURED = $00030047
#MBX_TAG_TEMPERATURE    = $00030006
#MBX_TAG_MAX_TEMPERATURE = $0003000A

; ---- the framebuffer tags --------------------------------------------
; ON DISK AND CITABLE, 2026-08-25. These were the seven numbers
; display.pi4 refused to write down: the defining header was not on this
; machine, two agents each burned a full filesystem search looking for
; it, and the library shipped with seven EMPTY SLOTS and a refusal
; (#DSP_ENOTAGS) rather than a guess. The download was then authorised.
;
; Source: Datasheets/pi4/raspberrypi-firmware.h - the kernel's
; include/soc/bcm2835/raspberrypi-firmware.h, enum
; rpi_firmware_property_tag. Line numbers given per value so each is
; checkable in seconds.
;
; They live HERE and not in display.pi4 because display.pi4's header
; says so in as many words: "put them in the main program, or in
; mailbox.pi4 beside the tags it already carries, and leave this file's
; slots as slots. Being correct about what it does not know is this
; file's job; being the home of a number whose provenance is a wiki page
; is not." The provenance is no longer a wiki page, and the slot
; mechanism is still the right shape - so the numbers came here.
;
; WHAT THIS DOWNLOAD ALSO SETTLED: the eight tags above were taken from
; the same header UPSTREAM and cross-checked against the firmware
; project's documentation. All eight now match the on-disk file value
; for value, including $00030047 and $0003000A. The cross-check was
; sound; it is simply citable now.
#MBX_TAG_FB_ALLOCATE   = $00040001   ; h:97   FRAMEBUFFER_ALLOCATE
#MBX_TAG_FB_GETPITCH   = $00040008   ; h:104  FRAMEBUFFER_GET_PITCH
#MBX_TAG_FB_SETPHYS    = $00048003   ; h:120  SET_PHYSICAL_WIDTH_HEIGHT
#MBX_TAG_FB_SETVIRT    = $00048004   ; h:121  SET_VIRTUAL_WIDTH_HEIGHT
#MBX_TAG_FB_SETDEPTH   = $00048005   ; h:122  SET_DEPTH
#MBX_TAG_FB_SETORDER   = $00048006   ; h:123  SET_PIXEL_ORDER
#MBX_TAG_FB_SETOFFSET  = $00048009   ; h:125  SET_VIRTUAL_OFFSET

; ---- three more of the same family, added 2026-08-26 ------------------
; U-Boot's combined framebuffer message carries nine tags and this file
; only had six of them. These are the missing three. Each now has TWO
; on-disk citations rather than one, because a second copy of the same
; header arrived with the U-Boot download and the two agree value for
; value - which is the bar the eight clock and temperature tags at the
; top of this section were held to and the framebuffer six were not:
;
;   h  = Datasheets/pi4/raspberrypi-firmware.h        (the kernel enum)
;   ub = Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_mbox.h         (U-Boot's #defines)
;
; The six above were re-checked against ub at the same time and all six
; match: ALLOCATE ub:270, GET_PITCH ub:410, SET_PHYSICAL ub:315,
; SET_VIRTUAL ub:335, SET_DEPTH ub:354, SET_PIXEL_ORDER ub:371,
; SET_VIRTUAL_OFFSET ub:426. They are no longer single-sourced.
#MBX_TAG_FB_GETPHYS    = $00040003   ; h:99   GET_PHYSICAL_WIDTH_HEIGHT
                                     ; ub:313 BCM2835_MBOX_TAG_GET_PHYSICAL_W_H
#MBX_TAG_FB_SETALPHA   = $00048007   ; h:124  SET_ALPHA_MODE
                                     ; ub:391 BCM2835_MBOX_TAG_SET_ALPHA_MODE
#MBX_TAG_FB_SETOVERSCAN = $0004800A  ; h:126  SET_OVERSCAN
                                     ; ub:445 BCM2835_MBOX_TAG_SET_OVERSCAN

; ---- and the VALUES those two tags carry ------------------------------
; THE TAG NUMBER AND THE MEANING OF ITS VALUE ARE TWO SEPARATE THINGS TO
; KNOW, and display.pi4 spent a whole section on that distinction: it
; refused to send set-pixel-order because the number became citable on
; 2026-08-25 and the meaning of the state word did not. The U-Boot
; header settles the second half as well, so the refusal is over. It is
; recorded here rather than quietly dropped, because a constraint that
; disappears without explanation gets re-derived by the next reader.
#MBX_FB_ORDER_BGR = 0          ; ub:373 BCM2835_MBOX_PIXEL_ORDER_BGR
#MBX_FB_ORDER_RGB = 1          ; ub:374 BCM2835_MBOX_PIXEL_ORDER_RGB

#MBX_FB_ALPHA_0_OPAQUE      = 0  ; ub:393 - alpha 0 means fully opaque
#MBX_FB_ALPHA_0_TRANSPARENT = 1  ; ub:394 - alpha 0 means fully clear
#MBX_FB_ALPHA_IGNORED       = 2  ; ub:395 - the channel is not looked at

; ======================================================================
;  THE DISPLAY SELECTION TAGS - ADDED 2026-08-26
; ======================================================================
;
;  THE GAP THEY CLOSE. A Pi 4 has TWO HDMI sockets. Until today nothing
;  in this tree ever told the firmware which one to put a framebuffer
;  on, so the firmware's own choice was silently accepted and could not
;  even be reported afterwards - the monitor's "no framebuffer" message
;  named HDMI0 out of nothing but habit, which sends anybody whose cable
;  is in HDMI1 to the wrong end of the board.
;
;  ---- A THIRD CITATION KEY, AND WHY THERE HAD TO BE ONE -------------
;
;  Every other number in this file carries "h:NN" or "ub:NNN". Those two
;  keys were enough because every other tag appears in both files. THESE
;  THREE APPEAR IN NEITHER:
;
;    Datasheets/pi4/raspberrypi-firmware.h   - the kernel enum this file
;      cites as h:. It is the UPSTREAM header, and upstream Linux has no
;      firmware-KMS driver, so it has no reason to carry the tags that
;      only that driver uses. Grepping it for DISPLAY_NUM returns
;      nothing.
;    Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_mbox.h    - cited as ub:. U-Boot sets
;      up ONE framebuffer on whatever the firmware offers and never
;      selects a display, so it has no display-selection tag either.
;
;  THAT ABSENCE IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS WAS MISSED. Two sources agreed
;  perfectly, and they agreed about a smaller world than the one the
;  board is in. Agreement between two documents is not coverage.
;
;  So two more keys, each naming a DIFFERENT file. The key is per-file
;  and there are now four of them; read the key before the line number:
;
;    h  = Datasheets/pi4/raspberrypi-firmware.h
;         the kernel's include/soc/bcm2835/raspberrypi-firmware.h
;    ub = Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_mbox.h
;         U-Boot v2025.01 arch/arm/mach-bcm283x/include/mach/mbox.h
;    dh = Datasheets/pi4/hdmi/raspberrypi-firmware-downstream.h
;         the SAME kernel header from the raspberrypi/linux DOWNSTREAM
;         tree, which is a superset: it carries the tags the Pi's own
;         drivers use and upstream's copy does not. THE TAG NUMBERS
;         BELOW COME FROM HERE AND FROM NOWHERE ELSE.
;    fk = Datasheets/pi4/hdmi/vc4_firmware_kms.c
;         the downstream DRM driver that actually sends them. It is not
;         a source for the numbers - it uses the symbols - but it is
;         the only source on this disk for HOW they are used, which for
;         these three is more than half of what has to be known.
;
;  SINGLE-SOURCED, AND SAID PLAINLY. The eight clock tags at the top of
;  this file were held to "two independent documents agree, value for
;  value". These three cannot meet that bar, because only one document
;  on this machine contains them at all. What they have instead is one
;  document for the NUMBER and a second, independent one for the
;  BEHAVIOUR - a driver that compiles against that header and ships on
;  this exact silicon. That is weaker than two headers agreeing and it
;  is written down here rather than glossed, so that if a display
;  selection ever behaves oddly on the bench this paragraph is the first
;  place to look.
;
;  ---- WHAT THE DRIVER ACTUALLY DOES WITH THEM ----------------------
;
;  Read out of fk, not reasoned about. This is the part that decides the
;  shape of every caller, so it is quoted rather than summarised:
;
;  1. GET_NUM_DISPLAYS is a message of its own, a pure GET with no
;     request words and one response word.
;       fk:1958-1960  rpi_firmware_property(..., GET_NUM_DISPLAYS,
;                                           &num_displays, sizeof(u32))
;     A FAILURE IS NOT FATAL THERE. fk:1962-1970 comments that a
;     firmware old enough to lack the call should be treated as having
;     one display, and carries on. Wrappers here take the same view:
;     "the firmware would not say" is a distinct answer from "zero".
;
;  2. GET_DISPLAY_ID is a message of its own, and it is IN AND OUT
;     THROUGH THE SAME WORD - the caller writes an INDEX, the firmware
;     answers with that display's identity.
;       fk:1990-1994  for (display_num = 0; display_num < num_displays;
;                          display_num++) {
;                       display_id = display_num;
;                       rpi_firmware_property(..., GET_DISPLAY_ID,
;                                             &display_id, ...);
;     So there are TWO NUMBERINGS and confusing them is the obvious way
;     to get this wrong. See THE TWO NUMBERINGS below.
;
;  3. SET_DISPLAY_NUM is NOT a message of its own. It is the FIRST TAG
;     of a message whose LATER TAGS are the ones it applies to:
;       fk:1845-1850  struct mailbox_blank_display blank = {
;                       .tag1 = {SET_DISPLAY_NUM, 4, 0, },
;                       .display = display_idx,
;                       .tag2 = {FRAMEBUFFER_BLANK, 4, 0, },
;                       .blank = 1,
;                     };
;       fk:1863       rpi_firmware_property_list(vc4->firmware, &blank,
;                                                sizeof(blank));
;     One buffer. Selection first, then the tag it steers. That is the
;     ONLY use of SET_DISPLAY_NUM anywhere in the driver.
;
;  ---- WHAT THE DRIVER DOES *NOT* SETTLE, AND IT MATTERS ------------
;
;  WHETHER THE SELECTION IS STICKY. The driver sends SET_DISPLAY_NUM
;  exactly once, in one two-tag message, and never relies on it again -
;  every other display-aware tag it sends carries the display number as
;  a FIELD OF ITS OWN VALUE BUFFER instead:
;    fk:915        set_plane.display     = display_num
;    fk:955        set_timings.display   = display_number
;    fk:1397       set_timings.display   = display_number  (GET_TIMING)
;    fk:1449-1450  mailbox_get_edid.display_number
;    fk:1812       mailbox_display_pwr.display
;  A driver that had a sticky, connection-wide "current display" would
;  not need five separate display fields. That is SUGGESTIVE of
;  message-scope and it is not proof, and no file on this disk says
;  either way: the firmware project's own property-interface
;  documentation (Datasheets/pi4/hdmi/firmware-Mailbox-property-
;  interface.md) does not document SET_DISPLAY_NUM, GET_NUM_DISPLAYS or
;  GET_DISPLAY_ID AT ALL - grep it for "display" and you get the
;  physical-size headings and a Pi-3 gamma tag.
;
;  WHAT GET_PHYSICAL_W_H DOES AFTERWARDS is unestablished for the same
;  reason: the driver never sends a framebuffer configuration tag in a
;  message with SET_DISPLAY_NUM, so nothing here demonstrates what the
;  combination does.
;
;  THE RULE THIS TREE THEREFORE FOLLOWS, chosen because it is correct
;  under BOTH answers rather than because one of them was picked:
;
;      EVERY MESSAGE THAT TOUCHES THE FRAMEBUFFER CARRIES THE SELECTION
;      AS ITS FIRST TAG, WHENEVER A SELECTION IS IN FORCE.
;
;  If the selection is message-scoped, that is required. If it is
;  sticky, re-sending the same index is a no-op. The one shape that is
;  wrong under either answer is the tempting one - send SET_DISPLAY_NUM
;  in a message of its own and assume the next message inherits it -
;  and this tree does not do it. display.pi4 implements the rule; see
;  DISPLAY SELECTION in that file.
;
;  ---- THE TWO NUMBERINGS --------------------------------------------
;
;  An INDEX is 0..num_displays-1, a position in the list of displays the
;  firmware found at boot. SET_DISPLAY_NUM takes an INDEX (fk:1847
;  passes display_idx, the loop counter) and GET_DISPLAY_ID takes an
;  INDEX (fk:1991 writes display_num into the value word).
;
;  An ID is the DispmanX display identity, which is FIXED per socket and
;  does not move when displays come and go. GET_DISPLAY_ID returns one.
;  fk:343-356 maps every id to a display type, and the comments in that
;  table are the only naming of the sockets on this disk. fk:344-346
;  heads it "The firmware display (DispmanX) IDs map to specific types
;  in a fixed manner", which is the sentence that makes an id safe to
;  print and an index not:
;
;      0  MAIN_LCD      DSI or DPI     fk:347
;      1  AUX_LCD       DSI            fk:348
;      2  HDMI0         TMDS           fk:349
;      3  VEC           composite      fk:350
;      4  FORCE_LCD                    fk:351
;      5  FORCE_TV                     fk:352
;      6  FORCE_OTHER                  fk:353
;      7  HDMI1         TMDS           fk:354
;      8  FORCE_TV2                    fk:355
;
;  fk:1140-1150 confirms 2 and 7 independently: it caps the pixel clock
;  per socket with "case 2: /* HDMI0 */" (fk:1141) and
;  "case 7: /* HDMI1 */" (fk:1146).
;
;  WITH ONE DISPLAY PLUGGED INTO HDMI1 THE INDEX IS 0 AND THE ID IS 7,
;  which is exactly the confusion this whole section exists to prevent:
;  index 0 does not mean HDMI0.
;
;  WHICH SOCKET IS "HDMI0" PHYSICALLY is not in any file on this disk.
;  The note above that it is the one nearer the USB-C power jack is Pi 4
;  board lore, not a citation, and it is marked as such here rather than
;  printed by anything.
; ======================================================================
; THE VALUE-BUFFER SIZES ARE THE DRIVER'S, not a guess: each is the
; sizeof() the driver hands rpi_firmware_property, or the buf_size in
; the tag header it builds by hand.
#MBX_TAG_FB_GETNUMDISPLAYS = $00040013  ; dh:124 FRAMEBUFFER_GET_NUM_DISPLAYS
                                        ; value buffer 4 bytes, nothing in,
                                        ; the count out. fk:1958-1960 passes
                                        ; sizeof(u32).
#MBX_TAG_FB_SETDISPLAYNUM  = $00048013  ; dh:123 FRAMEBUFFER_SET_DISPLAY_NUM
                                        ; value buffer 4 bytes, one u32 INDEX
                                        ; in. fk:1846 builds the tag header
                                        ; literally as {tag, 4, 0}.
#MBX_TAG_FB_GETDISPLAYID   = $00040016  ; dh:122 FRAMEBUFFER_GET_DISPLAY_ID
                                        ; value buffer 4 bytes, and the SAME
                                        ; WORD both ways: INDEX in, ID out.
                                        ; fk:1991-1994.
; dh:125 FRAMEBUFFER_GET_DISPLAY_SETTINGS = $00040014 is deliberately NOT
; declared. Its payload is undocumented everywhere on this disk; see
; DELIBERATELY LEFT OUT in the header for the argument.

; ---- and the VALUES those tags carry ---------------------------------
; The DispmanX display ids, from the table at fk:341-359 quoted above.
; These are IDS, never indices. Only the two HDMI sockets are named as
; constants because they are the two this board has and the two anything
; here has a use for; the rest are in the comment above, written down
; once, so that a program meeting id 3 can look it up instead of
; guessing.
; The two DSI-capable identifiers. Declared 2026-09-06 for the Waveshare
; panel work: on a Pi 4 the display connector's panel, IF the firmware
; brings it up at all, arrives as one of these and NOT as a new tag. The
; whole display-select path above is identifier-agnostic, so nothing else
; had to change - only the naming, so a census can say what it found
; instead of printing a bare number. See the vault note "Waveshare 10.1
; DSI and touch - what the hardware is".
;
; WHETHER EITHER EVER APPEARS ON THIS BOARD IS UNKNOWN and is exactly the
; experiment: the firmware's built-in DSI support is for the official
; Raspberry Pi Touch Display, which it detects on I2C, and a third-party
; panel is predicted NOT to be enumerated. Predicted, not measured.
#MBX_FB_DISPLAY_MAIN_LCD = 0   ; fk:347 display_types[0], /* MAIN_LCD - DSI or DPI */
#MBX_FB_DISPLAY_AUX_LCD = 1    ; fk:348 display_types[1], /* AUX_LCD */
#MBX_FB_DISPLAY_HDMI0 = 2      ; fk:349 display_types[2], /* HDMI0 */
                               ; and fk:1141 "case 2: /* HDMI0 */"
#MBX_FB_DISPLAY_HDMI1 = 7      ; fk:354 display_types[7], /* HDMI1 */
                               ; and fk:1146 "case 7: /* HDMI1 */"

; The alignment ALLOCATE_BUFFER is asked for. Not a protocol constant -
; it is the caller's request - but it is U-Boot's number and there is no
; reason to differ: v2025.01_msg.c:188, allocate_buffer.body.req.alignment
; = 0x100.
#MBX_FB_ALLOC_ALIGN = $100

; ---- clock ids --------------------------------------------------------
#MBX_CLK_EMMC = 1
#MBX_CLK_UART = 2
#MBX_CLK_ARM  = 3
#MBX_CLK_CORE = 4

; ---- error codes, from MailboxError() --------------------------------
#MBX_OK           = 0
#MBX_ERR_NOINIT   = 1          ; MailboxInit() was not called, or failed
#MBX_ERR_ADDRESS  = 2          ; buffer above 1 GiB - unreachable, see above
#MBX_ERR_TOOBIG   = 3          ; the value buffer asked for does not fit
#MBX_ERR_TXTIMEOUT = 4         ; mailbox 1 stayed FULL
#MBX_ERR_RXTIMEOUT = 5         ; mailbox 0 stayed EMPTY, or only ever
                               ; produced other channels' traffic
#MBX_ERR_ECHO     = 6          ; the reply named a different buffer
#MBX_ERR_STATUS   = 7          ; the firmware's response code was not SUCCESS
#MBX_ERR_TAG      = 8          ; the firmware did not mark the tag answered
                               ; - on a list, MailboxListFailedTag() says
                               ;   which one
#MBX_ERR_MODE     = 9          ; a single-tag call while a list is open, or
                               ; a list call with no list. Loud, because
                               ; the two paths share one buffer and mixing
                               ; them would send a half-built message.
#MBX_ERR_EMPTY    = 10         ; MailboxListSend() with no tags in the list

; How long any single wait is willing to spin. Both bounds are always
; live: the tick deadline is the honest one, the iteration count is the
; backstop for the case where CNTFRQ_EL0 reads zero because nothing set
; it. An unbounded spin on a device that never answers is a hang with no
; message, which is the one outcome this library refuses to have.
#MBX_TIMEOUT_MS = 1000
#MBX_SPIN_LIMIT = 200000000

; The buffer.
;
; IT WAS ONE CACHE LINE UNTIL 2026-08-26 AND IS NOW FOUR. The largest
; single-tag request is nine words - 5 header + 3 value for a
; set-clock-rate + 1 end tag = 36 bytes, rounded to 48 - and 64 was
; ample for that. The multi-tag path is what needed the room:
; display.pi4's combined framebuffer message reproduces U-Boot's
; struct msg_setup (v2025.01_msg.c:35-47), which is
;
;   8 header + 20 + 20 + 16 + 16 + 16 + 20 + 28 + 20 + 16 + 4 end
;
; = 184 bytes, rounded to 192. 256 leaves headroom for a tenth tag
; without another visit here, and it is FOUR WHOLE CACHE LINES, which is
; the property that actually matters.
;
; THE TENTH TAG ARRIVED THE SAME DAY, which is a nice demonstration that
; the headroom was worth leaving: display.pi4 puts SET_DISPLAY_NUM in
; front of those nine whenever a display has been chosen (12 header + 4
; value = 16 bytes, taking the message to 200) and in front of the
; get-physical query as well. Both still fit inside 256 with a whole
; cache line to spare. An ELEVENTH would not leave much, so the next
; person to add one should read the line-ownership argument below before
; assuming there is room.
;
; THE LINE-OWNERSHIP ARGUMENT IS UNCHANGED AND IS WHY 256 AND NOT 200.
; See THE WINDOW IS ALIGNED TO 64 in the header: the window must consist
; of whole 64-byte lines that belong to this buffer alone, because a
; line shared with a neighbouring global can be dirtied between the send
; and the reply and the clean afterwards would write that stale copy
; back over the firmware's answer. A 3.25-line window would reintroduce
; exactly that. Raise #MBX_MAX_BYTES only in multiples of 64, and raise
; #MBX_BUF_BYTES / #MBX_BUF_WORDS with it.
;
; The array is #MBX_MAX_BYTES + 64 so that an aligned window of the full
; size always fits inside it whatever the array's own base alignment
; turns out to be (worst case wastes 63 bytes at the front and 1 at the
; back).
#MBX_CACHE_LINE = 64
#MBX_BUF_WORDS = 80
#MBX_BUF_BYTES = 320
#MBX_MAX_BYTES = 256

; How many tags one composed message may carry. Nine is what U-Boot's
; framebuffer setup needs; twelve is that with room to spare and still
; small enough that the two index arrays are a rounding error in .bss.
; MailboxListAdd() refuses the thirteenth rather than writing past them.
#MBX_MAX_TAGS = 12

; Which half of the transport is currently holding the buffer. The two
; halves share one buffer on purpose - one alignment argument, one set
; of cache operations, one bounded wait - so they must not overlap, and
; the overlap is REFUSED rather than tolerated. A MailboxSend() issued
; while a list is half-built would transmit a buffer whose header says
; one thing and whose body says another.
#MBX_MODE_IDLE   = 0
#MBX_MODE_SINGLE = 1
#MBX_MODE_LIST   = 2

Global Dim mbxBuf.l[#MBX_BUF_WORDS]

Global mbxBufAddr.i            ; 64-byte-aligned ARM address inside mbxBuf
Global mbxBusAddr.i            ; the same buffer, named for the VideoCore
Global mbxReady.i              ; MailboxInit() succeeded
Global mbxErr.i                ; last error, one of #MBX_ERR_*
Global mbxTimeoutTicks.i       ; #MBX_TIMEOUT_MS in CNTPCT_EL0 ticks
Global mbxHaveClock.i          ; CNTFRQ_EL0 was non-zero
Global mbxTotalBytes.i         ; size of the request currently being built
Global mbxValueBytes.i         ; its value buffer
Global mbxFlushAddr.i          ; one cache line, for MbxCacheLine()

; ---- the multi-tag half ----------------------------------------------
Global mbxMode.i               ; #MBX_MODE_* - who owns the buffer
Global mbxListCount.i          ; tags added so far
Global mbxListCursor.i         ; byte offset of the next free tag slot
Global mbxListBad.i            ; index of the first tag the firmware left
                               ; unanswered, or -1
Global Dim mbxListOff.i[#MBX_MAX_TAGS]   ; byte offset of each tag header
Global Dim mbxListVal.i[#MBX_MAX_TAGS]   ; each tag's value buffer, bytes

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The architectural counter. CNTPCT_EL0 and CNTFRQ_EL0 are readable
;  with no setup and no trap at the exception levels a loader hands a
;  program, which makes them the cheapest honest clock a library with no
;  dependencies can have. armstub8.S sets CNTFRQ_EL0 from OSC_FREQ,
;  which is 54000000 on BCM2711 (armstub8.S:54 and :110-112), so on a
;  normally-booted Pi 4 this reads 54 MHz. It is READ rather than
;  assumed, because a program that arrived some other way may find it
;  zero - and that case is handled rather than divided by.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MbxTicks()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cntpct_el0
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MbxCounterHz()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cntfrq_el0
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  One 64-byte cache line, cleaned AND invalidated to the point of
;  coherency. "civac", not the Monitor's "cvau": cvau cleans to the
;  point of UNIFICATION, which is the right target when the reader is
;  this core's instruction fetch. The reader here is the VideoCore,
;  which is not on the far side of the point of unification but of the
;  point of COHERENCY, and it both reads what we wrote and writes what
;  we then read - so the line has to be pushed out and dropped.
;
;  "ldr x9", not "ldr w9". mbxFlushAddr is a .i and a .i is eight bytes
;  here; loading it with ldr w9 would silently lose the top half of an
;  address.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure MbxCacheLine()
  ASM
    adrp x9, global_mbxflushaddr
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_mbxflushaddr
    ldr  x9, [x9]
    dc civac, x9
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure MbxSyncBuffer()
  Define a.i
  Define e.i
  ; mbxBufAddr is on a 64-byte boundary and the window is #MBX_MAX_BYTES
  ; long, so this runs once per cache line in the window - FOUR times
  ; since 2026-08-26, when the window grew to hold a nine-tag message.
  ; The round-down was kept through the one-line era for exactly this
  ; moment and cost two instructions a transaction to have.
  ;
  ; THE WHOLE WINDOW, EVERY TIME, even for a message that fills 48 bytes
  ; of it. Cleaning a line that was not written is a few cycles and no
  ; effect; deciding which lines to clean from the message size would be
  ; a second place for the size accounting to be wrong, and getting it
  ; wrong leaves the firmware's answer sitting behind a stale cache line
  ; with nothing to announce it.
  ;
  ; Round down by shifting, not by masking: a mask written as a 32-bit
  ; literal would clear the top half of a 64-bit address as well as the
  ; low six bits. The address is physical and therefore positive, so the
  ; arithmetic shift is a logical one.
  a = (mbxBufAddr >> 6) << 6
  e = mbxBufAddr + #MBX_MAX_BYTES
  While a < e
    mbxFlushAddr = a
    MbxCacheLine()
    a = a + 64
  Wend
  a64_barrier()                ; dsb sy + isb
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Wait until (PeekL(reg) & bit) is clear, or give up. Returns 1 if the
;  bit cleared, 0 on timeout. Both deadlines are checked every pass; see
;  #MBX_TIMEOUT_MS.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MbxWaitClear(reg.i, bit.i)
  Define t0.i
  Define n.i
  t0 = MbxTicks()
  n = 0
  Repeat
    ; PeekL, not Peek: these are 32-bit registers and a bare Peek would
    ; read eight bytes. The & keeps only the bit asked for, so it does
    ; not matter whether the load sign-extended.
    If (PeekN(reg) & bit) = 0
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
    n = n + 1
    If n > #MBX_SPIN_LIMIT
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    If mbxHaveClock <> 0
      If (MbxTicks() - t0) > mbxTimeoutTicks
        ProcedureReturn 0
      EndIf
    EndIf
  ForEver
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  PUBLIC - set-up and status
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MailboxInit() - find a 16-byte-aligned buffer, prove the VideoCore
;  can name it, and work out the timeout. Returns 1 on success, 0 on the
;  single failure that is possible here: the buffer landed outside the
;  low gigabyte, where there is no bus alias for it. Call it once,
;  before anything else in this file.
;
;  It talks to no hardware, so it cannot fail for a hardware reason and
;  a 1 from it is NOT evidence the firmware is alive. The first
;  MailboxSend() is.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MailboxInit()
  Define hz.i

  mbxReady = 0
  mbxErr = #MBX_OK
  mbxTotalBytes = 0
  mbxValueBytes = 0
  mbxMode = #MBX_MODE_IDLE
  mbxListCount = 0
  mbxListCursor = 0
  mbxListBad = -1

  ; Round the array's base UP to the next 64-byte boundary. Sixteen is
  ; what the mailbox needs - the low four bits of the word are the
  ; channel, so an address with any of them set cannot be sent at all -
  ; and 64 is what the CACHE needs, so that this buffer owns its line
  ; and no neighbouring global can dirty it between the send and the
  ; reply. 64 satisfies 16, so one number does both jobs.
  ;
  ; -64 is a full-width 64-bit mask, which is why it is written as -64
  ; and not as a hex literal that would be 32 bits wide and would clear
  ; the top half of the address as well as the low six bits.
  mbxBufAddr = (@mbxBuf[0] + (#MBX_CACHE_LINE - 1)) & (-64)

  ; The dma-ranges alias is 0x40000000 long. Above that there is no name
  ; for this buffer that the VideoCore would understand, and forming one
  ; anyway would produce a valid-looking word pointing at the wrong
  ; memory. Refuse instead. See THE 1 GiB DMA WINDOW.
  If (mbxBufAddr + #MBX_MAX_BYTES) > #MBX_DMA_LIMIT
    mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_ADDRESS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  mbxBusAddr = (mbxBufAddr + #MBX_BUS_OFFSET) & $FFFFFFFF

  hz = MbxCounterHz()
  If hz > 0
    mbxHaveClock = 1
    ; Divide first. hz is tens of millions and the product with 1000
    ; would still fit a 64-bit .i, but dividing first is exact enough
    ; for a deadline and cannot overflow on any counter frequency.
    mbxTimeoutTicks = (hz / 1000) * #MBX_TIMEOUT_MS
  Else
    ; Nothing set CNTFRQ_EL0. Say so rather than dividing by it; the
    ; iteration backstop in MbxWaitClear() carries the deadline alone.
    mbxHaveClock = 0
    mbxTimeoutTicks = 0
  EndIf

  mbxReady = 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MailboxReady()
  ProcedureReturn mbxReady
EndProcedure

; The reason the last call failed, as one of the #MBX_ERR_* constants.
; Cleared to #MBX_OK at the start of every MailboxBegin() and every
; wrapper below, so it always describes the most recent attempt.
Procedure.i MailboxError()
  ProcedureReturn mbxErr
EndProcedure

; The ARM address of the aligned buffer, and the bus address the
; VideoCore was given. Diagnostics only - printing these two and
; checking the second is the first minus nothing but the offset is the
; quickest way to confirm the conversion on the bench.
Procedure.i MailboxBufferAddr()
  ProcedureReturn mbxBufAddr
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MailboxBusAddr()
  ProcedureReturn mbxBusAddr
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  PUBLIC - the transport. Build a single-tag request, send it, read the
;  answer. Every wrapper further down is three or four calls to these.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MailboxBegin(tag, reqBytes, respBytes) - lay out a one-tag property
;  buffer. reqBytes is how many bytes of value the request carries,
;  respBytes how many the firmware will write back; the value buffer is
;  sized to the larger, which is what the protocol requires and is why
;  a tag like set-clock-rate (12 in, 8 out) declares 12.
;
;  Returns 1 if the request fits, 0 otherwise. Fill the request words
;  with MailboxSetWord() afterwards, then call MailboxSend().
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MailboxBegin(tag.i, reqBytes.i, respBytes.i)
  Define valBytes.i
  Define total.i
  Define i.i

  mbxErr = #MBX_OK
  If mbxReady = 0
    mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_NOINIT
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  valBytes = reqBytes
  If respBytes > valBytes
    valBytes = respBytes
  EndIf
  ; Packets are processed a word at a time, so round the value buffer up
  ; to a multiple of four rather than letting a byte count through that
  ; would leave the end tag unaligned.
  valBytes = (valBytes + 3) & (-4)

  ; 5 header words + the value + the end tag, then rounded to 16 so the
  ; declared size never stops short of a word we wrote. The wiki's own
  ; description of the size field says it includes the padding.
  total = #MBX_OFF_VALUE + valBytes + 4
  total = (total + 15) & (-16)
  If total > #MBX_MAX_BYTES
    mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_TOOBIG
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  mbxTotalBytes = total
  mbxValueBytes = valBytes
  ; This call takes the buffer. Anything half-composed by the list path
  ; is discarded here rather than left to be mixed in later.
  mbxMode = #MBX_MODE_SINGLE
  mbxListCount = 0
  mbxListCursor = 0
  mbxListBad = -1

  ; Zero the whole thing first. The value words of a request that only
  ; fills some of them must not carry whatever the last call left, and
  ; the padding after the end tag should not either.
  i = 0
  While i < total
    PokeL(mbxBufAddr + i, 0)
    i = i + 4
  Wend

  PokeL(mbxBufAddr + #MBX_OFF_SIZE, total)
  PokeL(mbxBufAddr + #MBX_OFF_CODE, #MBX_REQUEST)
  PokeL(mbxBufAddr + #MBX_OFF_TAG, tag)
  PokeL(mbxBufAddr + #MBX_OFF_VALLEN, valBytes)
  ; The tag's own code word. The driver sets req_resp_size to 0 on the
  ; way out and reads the firmware's answer back out of the same word.
  PokeL(mbxBufAddr + #MBX_OFF_TAGCODE, 0)
  PokeL(mbxBufAddr + #MBX_OFF_VALUE + valBytes, #MBX_END_TAG)

  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; Word i of the value buffer, counted from zero.
Procedure MailboxSetWord(i.i, v.i)
  If mbxTotalBytes > 0
    If (i * 4) < mbxValueBytes
      PokeL(mbxBufAddr + #MBX_OFF_VALUE + (i * 4), v)
    EndIf
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MbxTransact() - hand the buffer to the firmware and wait for it back.
;
;  EVERYTHING THAT IS TRUE OF ANY PROPERTY MESSAGE, whether it carries
;  one tag or nine, and nothing that is true of only one of them. The
;  per-tag response-bit check is NOT here, because "which tags were
;  answered" is the one question the two paths answer differently:
;  MailboxSend() has one word to look at, MailboxListSend() has a list
;  and has to say which member failed.
;
;  Factored out of MailboxSend() on 2026-08-26 when the list path
;  arrived. Two copies of a mailbox transaction is two places for the
;  echo check, the channel filter or a deadline to be got wrong, and the
;  header's whole argument for this file is that the transport is the
;  hard, verifiable part.
;
;  The sequence, and every step of it is a check rather than a hope:
;    push the buffer out of the D-cache      (the VideoCore reads DRAM)
;    wait for mailbox 1 to be not-FULL       (bounded)
;    write channel 8 | bus address           (one word, both fields)
;    wait for mailbox 0 to be not-EMPTY      (bounded)
;    read, and if the low nibble is not 8 that reply belongs to someone
;      else - drop it and keep waiting, still bounded
;    check the upper 28 bits are OUR buffer, not another request's
;    pull the buffer back into the D-cache   (the VideoCore wrote DRAM)
;    check the response code says SUCCESS
;
;  Returns 1 only if all of that held. On 0, MailboxError() says which
;  step failed, and the value words must not be read.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MbxTransact()
  Define msg.i
  Define reply.i
  Define code.i
  Define n.i

  MbxSyncBuffer()

  ; MBOX_MSG(chan, data28): the address supplies bits 31:4 and the
  ; channel bits 3:0. mbxBusAddr is 64-byte aligned by construction, so
  ; the or cannot disturb the address; the mask is belt and braces and
  ; documents the field boundary at the one place the word is built.
  msg = (mbxBusAddr & (-16)) | (#MBX_CHANNEL & #MBX_CHANMASK)

  If MbxWaitClear(#MBX_STATUS1, #MBX_FULL) = 0
    mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_TXTIMEOUT
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  a64_barrier()
  PokeL(#MBX_WRITE, msg)
  a64_barrier()

  ; The read loop. A reply on another channel is not an error and not
  ; ours - drop it and go round. Bounded by the same spin limit so a
  ; firmware that only ever talks to somebody else still ends in a
  ; timeout rather than a hang.
  n = 0
  Repeat
    If MbxWaitClear(#MBX_STATUS0, #MBX_EMPTY) = 0
      mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_RXTIMEOUT
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    reply = PeekN(#MBX_READ) & $FFFFFFFF
    If (reply & #MBX_CHANMASK) = #MBX_CHANNEL
      Break
    EndIf
    n = n + 1
    If n > 64
      mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_RXTIMEOUT
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  ForEver

  a64_barrier()

  ; The firmware echoes the address it was given. If it is not the one
  ; we sent, this reply answers a different request and its buffer is
  ; not ours to read.
  If (reply & (-16) & $FFFFFFFF) <> (msg & (-16) & $FFFFFFFF)
    mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_ECHO
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; The VideoCore wrote DRAM under us; drop any stale lines before
  ; reading the answer back.
  MbxSyncBuffer()

  ; & $FFFFFFFF because SUCCESS is $80000000: bit 31 is set, and whether
  ; a 32-bit load sign-extended decides whether the comparison would
  ; otherwise be against a negative number. See the LANGUAGE MODEL note.
  code = PeekN(mbxBufAddr + #MBX_OFF_CODE) & $FFFFFFFF
  If code <> #MBX_SUCCESS
    mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_STATUS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MailboxSend() - send the single-tag request MailboxBegin() laid out.
;
;  UNCHANGED IN BEHAVIOUR by the 2026-08-26 multi-tag work. The body
;  moved into MbxTransact() and the per-tag check stayed here; the
;  #MBX_ERR_MODE guard below cannot fire for any caller that has not
;  opened a list, and nothing that existed before that date has.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MailboxSend()
  Define tagcode.i

  mbxErr = #MBX_OK
  If mbxReady = 0
    mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_NOINIT
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If mbxTotalBytes = 0
    mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_NOINIT
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; The list path owns the buffer right now. Refuse rather than transmit
  ; a half-composed message under a single-tag header.
  If mbxMode <> #MBX_MODE_SINGLE
    mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_MODE
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  If MbxTransact() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; A whole-buffer SUCCESS is not the same claim as "this tag was
  ; understood". The firmware marks each tag it answered by setting bit
  ; 31 of the tag's own code word; a tag it did not recognise is left
  ; clear while the buffer still reports success.
  tagcode = PeekN(mbxBufAddr + #MBX_OFF_TAGCODE) & $FFFFFFFF
  If (tagcode & #MBX_RESP_BIT) = 0
    mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_TAG
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; Word i of the value buffer after a successful send, masked to 32 bits
; so a rate at or above 2.147 GHz does not come back negative.
Procedure.i MailboxWord(i.i)
  If mbxTotalBytes = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (i * 4) >= mbxValueBytes
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(mbxBufAddr + #MBX_OFF_VALUE + (i * 4)) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

; How many bytes of value the firmware says it wrote. Usually the
; respBytes that was asked for; worth reading when it is not.
Procedure.i MailboxRespBytes()
  If mbxTotalBytes = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(mbxBufAddr + #MBX_OFF_TAGCODE) & #MBX_LEN_MASK
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  PUBLIC - SEVERAL TAGS IN ONE MESSAGE
;
;  Read SEVERAL TAGS IN ONE MESSAGE in the header first. The short
;  version: the firmware accumulates a device's requested state WITHIN
;  ONE property transaction and acts on what that transaction
;  accumulated. For the framebuffer that is not an optimisation, it is
;  the difference between a 1920 x 1080 surface and a 2 x 2 one.
;
;      MailboxListBegin()               take the buffer, empty the list
;      i = MailboxListAdd(tag, req, resp)   append one tag, -1 if it
;                                           will not fit
;      MailboxListSetWord(i, w, v)      fill request word w of tag i
;      MailboxListSend()                send once, validate everything
;      MailboxListWord(i, w)            response word w of tag i
;
;  The tags go out IN THE ORDER THEY WERE ADDED and that order is part
;  of the protocol, not a detail: the firmware processes them in buffer
;  order, so a tag that configures must precede the tag that acts on the
;  configuration. This file does not police the order - it cannot know
;  what a caller's tags mean - but it does not reorder them either, and
;  MailboxListAdd() returning a sequential index is the caller's handle
;  on that fact.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MailboxListBegin() - start composing. Returns 1, or 0 if the
;  transport is not up.
;
;  It writes the buffer header immediately and leaves the cursor just
;  past it. The total size word is NOT final until MailboxListSend()
;  writes it, because it is not known until the last tag is in.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MailboxListBegin()
  mbxErr = #MBX_OK
  If mbxReady = 0
    mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_NOINIT
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; Take the buffer away from the single-tag path. MailboxWord() and
  ; MailboxSetWord() both key off mbxTotalBytes, so zeroing it makes a
  ; stale single-tag read return 0 instead of a word out of somebody
  ; else's message.
  mbxTotalBytes = 0
  mbxValueBytes = 0
  mbxMode = #MBX_MODE_LIST
  mbxListCount = 0
  mbxListCursor = #MBX_HDR_BYTES
  mbxListBad = -1

  PokeL(mbxBufAddr + #MBX_OFF_SIZE, 0)
  PokeL(mbxBufAddr + #MBX_OFF_CODE, #MBX_REQUEST)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MailboxListAdd(tag, reqBytes, respBytes) - append one tag.
;
;  RETURNS THE TAG'S INDEX, 0 for the first, and -1 if it will not fit
;  or the list is not open. -1 and not 0, because 0 is a legitimate
;  index and a caller that ignored a failure would otherwise write its
;  words into the first tag.
;
;  reqBytes / respBytes have the same meaning as MailboxBegin()'s: the
;  value buffer is sized to the larger of the two, which is what the
;  protocol requires. v2025.01_mbox.h:96 says the same thing in C -
;  val_buf_size = sizeof(body), and body is a union of the request and
;  response structs.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MailboxListAdd(tag.i, reqBytes.i, respBytes.i)
  Define valBytes.i
  Define need.i
  Define total.i
  Define at.i
  Define i.i

  mbxErr = #MBX_OK
  If mbxReady = 0
    mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_NOINIT
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If mbxMode <> #MBX_MODE_LIST
    mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_MODE
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If mbxListCount >= #MBX_MAX_TAGS
    mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_TOOBIG
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf

  valBytes = reqBytes
  If respBytes > valBytes
    valBytes = respBytes
  EndIf
  ; "Packets are processed a dword at a time" - raspberrypi-firmware.c:99
  ; rejects a whole buffer whose size is not a multiple of four. Rounding
  ; each value buffer up to four is what keeps every LATER tag header
  ; word-aligned as well, which is the part that would not announce
  ; itself.
  valBytes = (valBytes + 3) & (-4)

  need = #MBX_TAGHDR_BYTES + valBytes
  ; The end tag has to fit too, and the whole buffer is rounded to 16.
  total = mbxListCursor + need + 4
  total = (total + 15) & (-16)
  If total > #MBX_MAX_BYTES
    mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_TOOBIG
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf

  at = mbxListCursor
  PokeL(mbxBufAddr + at + #MBX_TOFF_ID, tag)
  PokeL(mbxBufAddr + at + #MBX_TOFF_VALLEN, valBytes)
  ; U-Boot's BCM2835_MBOX_INIT_TAG (v2025.01_mbox.h:94-98) puts the
  ; REQUEST's length in this word on the way out; the kernel puts zero
  ; (raspberrypi-firmware.c:170). Both are in daily use against real
  ; firmware. This path follows U-Boot - see the long note in the header
  ; for why the two paths deliberately differ here rather than one of
  ; them being changed to match the other.
  ;
  ; Rounded to four for the same reason the value buffer is: a length
  ; that is not a whole number of words describes a buffer this protocol
  ; cannot address.
  PokeL(mbxBufAddr + at + #MBX_TOFF_CODE, (reqBytes + 3) & (-4))

  ; Zero the value words. A tag that fills only some of them must not
  ; carry whatever the last message left in the rest - and for a pure
  ; GET, which fills none, this is the whole of the request.
  i = 0
  While i < valBytes
    PokeL(mbxBufAddr + at + #MBX_TOFF_VALUE + i, 0)
    i = i + 4
  Wend

  mbxListOff[mbxListCount] = at
  mbxListVal[mbxListCount] = valBytes
  mbxListCursor = at + need
  mbxListCount = mbxListCount + 1
  ProcedureReturn mbxListCount - 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MailboxListSetWord(tagIndex, i, v) - word i of tag tagIndex's value
;  buffer, counted from zero. Silently does nothing for an out-of-range
;  index, which is MailboxSetWord()'s behaviour and the same argument:
;  the write cannot land anywhere harmful, and the tag's response will
;  not carry what the caller expected, which is the loud part.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure MailboxListSetWord(tagIndex.i, i.i, v.i)
  If tagIndex >= 0 And tagIndex < mbxListCount
    If (i * 4) < mbxListVal[tagIndex]
      PokeL(mbxBufAddr + mbxListOff[tagIndex] + #MBX_TOFF_VALUE + (i * 4), v)
    EndIf
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MailboxListSend() - close the buffer and send it, once.
;
;  On top of everything MbxTransact() checks, this checks EVERY TAG'S
;  OWN RESPONSE BIT, not just the first. That matters more here than it
;  does for a single tag: a combined message where eight tags were
;  understood and the ninth was not comes back with a whole-buffer
;  SUCCESS, and the eight that worked have already reconfigured the
;  device the ninth was supposed to act on. Reporting that as success
;  would be the exact failure this library exists to refuse.
;
;  Returns 1 or 0; on 0, MailboxError() names the step and
;  MailboxListFailedTag() names the tag when the step was #MBX_ERR_TAG.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MailboxListSend()
  Define total.i
  Define i.i
  Define code.i

  mbxErr = #MBX_OK
  If mbxReady = 0
    mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_NOINIT
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If mbxMode <> #MBX_MODE_LIST
    mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_MODE
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If mbxListCount = 0
    ; An empty list is not a message with no effect, it is a caller that
    ; thinks it added tags. Say so.
    mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_EMPTY
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  mbxListBad = -1

  PokeL(mbxBufAddr + mbxListCursor, #MBX_END_TAG)
  total = mbxListCursor + 4
  total = (total + 15) & (-16)
  If total > #MBX_MAX_BYTES
    ; MailboxListAdd() already refused anything that would land here, so
    ; reaching this means the accounting disagrees with itself. Refuse
    ; rather than send a size word that overruns the buffer.
    mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_TOOBIG
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; Zero the padding between the end tag and the declared size. The wiki
  ; and raspberrypi-firmware.c:114 both include the padding in the size,
  ; so the firmware will read it, and it must not read a word left over
  ; from a previous message and mistake it for a tag.
  i = mbxListCursor + 4
  While i < total
    PokeL(mbxBufAddr + i, 0)
    i = i + 4
  Wend
  PokeL(mbxBufAddr + #MBX_OFF_SIZE, total)
  PokeL(mbxBufAddr + #MBX_OFF_CODE, #MBX_REQUEST)

  If MbxTransact() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; Every tag, in order, so the FIRST one the firmware did not answer is
  ; the one reported - which is also the earliest point the message
  ; stopped meaning what the caller wrote.
  i = 0
  While i < mbxListCount
    code = PeekN(mbxBufAddr + mbxListOff[i] + #MBX_TOFF_CODE) & $FFFFFFFF
    If (code & #MBX_RESP_BIT) = 0
      mbxListBad = i
      mbxErr = #MBX_ERR_TAG
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    i = i + 1
  Wend

  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; Word i of tag tagIndex's value buffer after a successful send, masked
; to 32 bits so a framebuffer bus address of $DE400000 does not come
; back negative. Returns 0 for an index that is not there.
Procedure.i MailboxListWord(tagIndex.i, i.i)
  If tagIndex < 0 Or tagIndex >= mbxListCount
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (i * 4) >= mbxListVal[tagIndex]
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(mbxBufAddr + mbxListOff[tagIndex] + #MBX_TOFF_VALUE + (i * 4)) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

; How many bytes of value the firmware says it wrote for that tag.
Procedure.i MailboxListRespBytes(tagIndex.i)
  If tagIndex < 0 Or tagIndex >= mbxListCount
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(mbxBufAddr + mbxListOff[tagIndex] + #MBX_TOFF_CODE) & #MBX_LEN_MASK
EndProcedure

; How many tags are in the list.
Procedure.i MailboxListCount()
  ProcedureReturn mbxListCount
EndProcedure

; The index of the first tag the firmware did not mark answered, or -1.
; Only meaningful after a MailboxListSend() that returned 0 with
; MailboxError() = #MBX_ERR_TAG.
Procedure.i MailboxListFailedTag()
  ProcedureReturn mbxListBad
EndProcedure

; The byte offset of a tag inside the message. Diagnostics only - a
; harness that wants to check the buffer LAYOUT rather than its answers
; needs this, and computing it outside would mean a second copy of the
; sizing rules.
Procedure.i MailboxListTagOffset(tagIndex.i)
  If tagIndex < 0 Or tagIndex >= mbxListCount
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn mbxListOff[tagIndex]
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  PUBLIC - the clock tags
;
;  All four getters share one shape: request is the clock id, response
;  is the clock id echoed back and the rate in Hz. Word 1 is the rate.
;
;  A ZERO RATE IS THE FIRMWARE'S "I DO NOT KNOW", not an error - the
;  documentation says the rate comes back 0 for a clock that does not
;  exist on this part. These procedures return 0 for that AND for a
;  failed transaction, so check MailboxError() when 0 matters: #MBX_OK
;  with a zero rate means the firmware answered and said zero.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i MbxClockQuery(tag.i, clockId.i)
  If MailboxBegin(tag, 4, 8) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  MailboxSetWord(0, clockId)
  If MailboxSend() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn MailboxWord(1)
EndProcedure

; The rate the firmware has the clock CONFIGURED at, in Hz.
Procedure.i MailboxClockRate(clockId.i)
  ProcedureReturn MbxClockQuery(#MBX_TAG_GET_CLOCK, clockId)
EndProcedure

; The highest rate the firmware will accept for this clock.
Procedure.i MailboxClockRateMax(clockId.i)
  ProcedureReturn MbxClockQuery(#MBX_TAG_MAX_CLOCK, clockId)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MailboxClockRateMin(clockId.i)
  ProcedureReturn MbxClockQuery(#MBX_TAG_MIN_CLOCK, clockId)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The MEASURED rate, which is a different question from the configured
;  one and is the honest answer to "what is this thing actually doing".
;  The configured rate is what the firmware intends; the measured rate
;  is what it observes. They disagree whenever the governor has dropped
;  the core for thermal or undervoltage reasons, and that disagreement
;  is exactly what you want to see after a MailboxArmClockSet().
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MailboxClockRateMeasured(clockId.i)
  ProcedureReturn MbxClockQuery(#MBX_TAG_CLOCK_MEASURED, clockId)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MailboxClockRateSet(clockId, hz, skipTurbo)
;
;  RETURNS THE RATE THE FIRMWARE ACTUALLY APPLIED, in Hz, and 0 if the
;  transaction failed. That return value is the entire point of this
;  procedure, and it is not a courtesy:
;
;  THE FIRMWARE DOES NOT REFUSE A RATE IT DOES NOT LIKE. IT CLAMPS IT,
;  AND REPORTS SUCCESS. Ask for 2.5 GHz on a part whose max-clock-rate
;  is 1.5 GHz and the response code is SUCCESS, the tag is marked
;  answered, and the rate word in the reply says 1500000000. A wrapper
;  that returned the rate it was ASKED for - or just a 1 for "worked" -
;  would be a confident wrong answer about the speed of the machine, and
;  every timing calculation downstream would inherit it. So the caller
;  gets the firmware's number and can compare it with its own.
;
;  Three other things bound what you will get back, and none of them
;  produce an error either:
;    * the ceiling is arm_freq / arm_freq_max in config.txt, reported by
;      MailboxClockRateMax(). Raising the CPU past the board default
;      means editing config.txt; this tag cannot exceed what the
;      firmware was configured to allow.
;    * the on-demand governor moves the ARM clock between min and max by
;      itself. A rate set here can be walked back to the minimum a
;      moment later with no notification at all - which is why the
;      answer to "did it stick" is MailboxClockRateMeasured(), taken a
;      little while afterwards, and not this return value.
;    * undervoltage and over-temperature throttling override everything
;      above.
;
;  skipTurbo: pass 0 for normal behaviour. Non-zero asks the firmware
;  not to move the other turbo-managed clocks (core, sdram, v3d) along
;  with this one. It is the third request word in the documented layout.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MailboxClockRateSet(clockId.i, hz.i, skipTurbo.i)
  If MailboxBegin(#MBX_TAG_SET_CLOCK, 12, 8) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  MailboxSetWord(0, clockId)
  MailboxSetWord(1, hz)
  MailboxSetWord(2, skipTurbo)
  If MailboxSend() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; Word 1 of the RESPONSE - the applied rate. Never hz.
  ProcedureReturn MailboxWord(1)
EndProcedure

; ---- the ARM clock, which is what all of this was built for ----------
; Three one-line wrappers, so the clock id is written once and the
; question a program actually asks is spelled the way it is asked.

Procedure.i MailboxArmClock()
  ProcedureReturn MailboxClockRate(#MBX_CLK_ARM)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MailboxArmClockMax()
  ProcedureReturn MailboxClockRateMax(#MBX_CLK_ARM)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MailboxArmClockMeasured()
  ProcedureReturn MailboxClockRateMeasured(#MBX_CLK_ARM)
EndProcedure

; Returns the rate the firmware applied. Read the long comment on
; MailboxClockRateSet() before believing any number this hands back.
Procedure.i MailboxArmClockSet(hz.i)
  ProcedureReturn MailboxClockRateSet(#MBX_CLK_ARM, hz, 0)
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  PUBLIC - board identity and temperature
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The board revision word. This is the packed new-style revision code
;  the firmware reports - it is NOT a small integer, and decoding its
;  bitfields into a model name is a table this library deliberately does
;  not carry: the tag number is cited, a model table would not be.
;  Returns 0 on failure; check MailboxError().
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Linux reset-raspberrypi.c and U-Boot arch/arm/mach-bcm283x/msg.c:
; NOTIFY_XHCI_RESET reloads VL805 firmware after a PCI fundamental reset.
; https://github.com/raspberrypi/linux/blob/rpi-6.6.y/drivers/reset/reset-raspberrypi.c
; https://github.com/u-boot/u-boot/blob/master/arch/arm/mach-bcm283x/msg.c
; Uses the existing owned property buffer and preserves MailboxError on failure.
Procedure.i MailboxNotifyVl805Reset()
  If MailboxBegin($00030058, 4, 4) = 0 : ProcedureReturn 0 : EndIf
  MailboxSetWord(0, $00100000)
  ProcedureReturn MailboxSend()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MailboxBoardRevision()
  ; Request length 0 - this tag takes no argument - but the response is
  ; four bytes, so the value buffer is sized for the response.
  If MailboxBegin(#MBX_TAG_BOARD_REVISION, 0, 4) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If MailboxSend() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn MailboxWord(0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The SoC temperature, IN THOUSANDTHS OF A DEGREE CELSIUS - so 48732
;  means 48.732 C. It is not degrees and it is not tenths; the unit is
;  the documented one and converting it here would only hide it.
;  Temperature id 0 is the only sensor on this part.
;  Returns 0 on failure; check MailboxError(), since 0 millidegrees is
;  not a temperature this board will ever report.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MailboxTemperature()
  If MailboxBegin(#MBX_TAG_TEMPERATURE, 4, 8) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  MailboxSetWord(0, 0)
  If MailboxSend() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn MailboxWord(1)
EndProcedure

; The temperature at which the firmware starts throttling, same unit.
; Worth reading next to MailboxTemperature() before an overclock: the
; gap between them is how much headroom the clock request has.
Procedure.i MailboxTemperatureMax()
  If MailboxBegin(#MBX_TAG_MAX_TEMPERATURE, 4, 8) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  MailboxSetWord(0, 0)
  If MailboxSend() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn MailboxWord(1)
EndProcedure
